00:02:36.562 --> 00:02:38.020
Actually, everything
we talk about,

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.840
we'll use in later in the class.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.330
And remember, that
I said that there's

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.330
a whole industry
of people coming up

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.640
with various extensions
of convexity.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.670
And there's pseudoconvexity
and quasiconvexity

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:53.870
and log convexity.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.137
We'll talk about that.

00:02:55.137 --> 00:02:55.970
All sorts of things.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.830
And there's a whole
bunch of these things

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:00.630
and whole books written on them
and all that kind of stuff.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.190
So we have selected a
small subset of these

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:05.630
that are actually useful.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.330
Quasiconvex, which I talked
about last time, is one.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.250
And the other one,
which is interesting,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.920
comes up in a lot of
fields, but especially

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.340
in statistics is the idea of
log-concave and log-convex

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:17.840
functions.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.880
And that's what we're going
to talk about today first.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.970
So what is it?

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:26.190
Actually, it's
super-duper simple.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:28.820
We just say that a function,
which has to be positive,

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.710
is log-concave.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:35.150
If its log, it's concave,
so that's all it means.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.520
Sorry, yes, log-concave.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.560
Now there's a weird
asymmetry here.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.760
And it turns out it's
log-concave things that come up

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.530
all the time, not log-convex.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:49.400
So if you do a google
search on log-concave,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.420
you'll get zillions of papers
and books and things like that.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.505
Log-convex, you'll
get almost nothing.

00:03:57.505 --> 00:03:58.880
I don't know the
reason for that,

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.010
but there's a weird asymmetry.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:05.390
So what that means is if you
take the Jensen inequality that

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:09.200
defines convexity for the log,
and then you exponentiate it,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.002
then you get this.

00:04:10.002 --> 00:04:11.460
And it's actually
real interesting.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:15.830
It says that a log-concave
function, when you evaluate it,

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.260
it at a mixture of arguments--

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:22.850
that's theta x plus
1 minus theta y--

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.010
if it were concave,
this would simply

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:29.450
be bigger than or equal
to the weighted arithmetic

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:31.250
mean of f of x and f of y.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.050
That's what it
means to be concave.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:37.020
Here, this is the
weighted geometric mean.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:38.150
That's what people call it.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:40.400
So it's the weighted
geometric mean.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.990
So, for example,
if theta is 1/2,

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:46.100
it's literally the geometric
mean of the two values.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.690
I mean, this is just
repeating stuff.

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:55.510
So there's a bunch of
examples, like these powers are

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:57.220
log-convex if a is negative.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.980
And they're
log-concave for a being

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:03.010
bigger than or equal to 0.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.290
So that's one.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:06.760
But mostly, it
comes up in things

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.500
like probability densities.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:15.295
So a good example is here's
a normal density on rn.

00:05:15.295 --> 00:05:18.430
So that's the density of
a standard normal random

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:19.790
variable.

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.315
And here, if you
take a look at this,

00:05:22.315 --> 00:05:25.420
this thing is log-concave.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:26.600
Let's check that.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.350
Well, if I take the log of
it, this is just a constant.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:33.145
And it's completely
irrelevant for curvature.

00:05:33.145 --> 00:05:35.020
And then over here, you
take the log of that,

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.000
and you get a
negative quadratic.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.740
Sigma is positive definite.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:42.160
So this thing here is
going to be concave.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.500
And I'm including
the minus sign here.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.790
So that's your canonical
example of a log.

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:52.370
And in fact, if you
have a measure that

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:53.840
has a density
that's log-concave,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.820
it's called a
log-concave measure.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:57.380
And they're
super-duper important

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:59.060
in lots of different fields.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.840
And it comes up in economics,
it comes up in statistics a lot.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:06.067
And, again, this is just
a more advanced topic,

00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:07.400
you don't have to know about it.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.620
It's, for example, a
very good regularizer.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.070
Like, if you say, I'm going to
fit a distribution to my data

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.020
and you insist that
distribution be log-concave,

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:17.870
that's actually a very
sensible regularizer.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:20.480
Again, this is
just for people who

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.600
know what I'm talking about.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.310
By the way, a ton of common
densities are log-concave.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:34.460
I actually don't remember which
ones, but it's a long list.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:38.870
Probably most or at least
half of all named densities

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.070
are log-concave.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:43.430
And it's not very hard for you
to figure out which is which,

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.550
so those are some examples.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:47.300
Here's one.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:51.390
This one is not obvious at all.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:53.990
There is going to be one new
thing in this topic, which

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:56.310
I'll get to shortly.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:58.073
But this is not obvious at all.

00:06:58.073 --> 00:06:59.615
So here's the
cumulative distribution

00:06:59.615 --> 00:07:04.850
of a standard Gaussian
distribution on R. So

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.710
that's the CDF.

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:10.490
Now, of course,
there's libraries

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.720
that compute this and
all that sort of stuff.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:14.400
I mean, obviously.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.750
But this is not obvious at
all, that this is actually

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:18.800
log-concave.

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.810
In fact, that's a
homework exercise.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.420
And it's not a short
one, and it's got hints.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:29.773
So it's not totally
obvious, but it's true.

00:07:29.773 --> 00:07:31.190
Actually, we're
going to see later

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:36.230
that any CDF of any
cumulative distribution

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:40.520
function of a
log-concave density

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.575
is going to be also log-concave.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.710
So here are some properties.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.290
This just translates.

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.540
If you write out
the formula for what

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:58.500
the Hessian of the
log of f is, and you

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.850
say that's less than or equal
to 0, in a matrix sense,

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:05.790
then you take the x, and you get
something that looks like that.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.160
And it's actually interesting.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.590
Remember, f is
positive, so this says

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:13.900
that a positive
multiple of the Hessian

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.740
is less than or equal
to, in a matrix sense,

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.240
the outer product
of the gradient.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.043
Now that's rank one.

00:08:22.043 --> 00:08:23.460
And, again, this
is more advanced,

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.640
and we probably won't need this,
but I don't mind saying it.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.470
When a matrix is less than or
equal to, in a rank 1 matrix,

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.169
it says that this
thing can have at most

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:37.350
one positive eigenvalue.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.190
That's what it says.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.740
And it says, all the others are
going to have to be negative.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:44.700
So that's the condition.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.620
So some things are
obvious, like product

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.370
of log-concave functions
is log-concave.

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:50.290
That's obvious.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:53.740
Because if I take two
log-concave functions,

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:54.730
I multiply them.

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:57.200
Now I take the log, it's
just the sum of the logs.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.220
So it relies on the deep
fact that the sum of two

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.110
concave functions is concave.

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:05.410
So this one is easy.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:08.950
Now the sum of
log-concave functions

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:10.540
is not always log-concave.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:15.330
In fact, someone want to give
me a quick example using maybe

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:18.870
the stuff we just looked at?

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.430
Set of two Gaussian densities?

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:27.780
Somebody want to--
all right, here we go.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:37.730
Here's one Gaussian,
and here's another one.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:39.350
They're both log-concave.

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.480
If I sum those, what do I get?

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.530
I get some function that
goes up, has a bump,

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:48.380
comes back down, it
goes up again, and down.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:51.460
Could that be log-concave?

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:55.120
It could not be
because, well, one way

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:58.720
to say it is I
could look at it, I

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.260
could say it's not
quasiconcave, for example.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:07.990
Because it's got a portion
where it bends down,

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:10.300
so that's not log-concave.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:15.160
I'll let you flesh out
that example on the board.

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:21.280
And then it turns out
there is a nontrivial fact

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:23.560
about log-concave functions.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:27.700
As far as I know, this
wasn't known until 1973,

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:30.460
whereas most of the other
things we've talked about

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.740
were known maybe
in the 1920s, '30s.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:34.660
Or maybe no one really
asked the question.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:37.540
And it was so simple that had
we asked people in the 1920s,

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:39.430
they would have known.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:41.960
I believe this to not
be the case for this.

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:43.270
So the rule is this.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:48.190
It says that if you have a
function that's log-concave

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:52.730
in two variables and you
integrate out over 1--

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:53.795
so that's this thing--

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.790
then the result is log-concave.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.860
So that's the integration rule.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:06.590
It's not obvious.

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:09.240
It's not simple to prove.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.110
I mean, it's not
hugely complicated,

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.430
but it's not simple.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:15.120
And it's cool.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.670
It's relatively recent.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:19.170
By the way, this
immediately-- well,

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:20.420
we'll get to that in a second.

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:21.720
There are some examples.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:26.550
So that tells you
that log-concavity

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:29.490
is preserved under convolution.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:31.260
So this is the convolution.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:41.790
And it says that if f and
g are log-concave, then

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:42.930
their convolution is.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.390
And so, in probability
what this tells

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.880
you is if I have two
random variables, each

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:51.840
with a log-concave
density, and I add them,

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:56.950
then that distribution
is also log-concave.

00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.080
So that's what that tells you.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:04.590
Here's a super interesting one.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:11.580
If I have a convex set in Rn,
and I have a random variable

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:14.940
with a log-concave density.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:18.780
Then if I look at the
probability that x plus y

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:24.585
is in C, that is a
log-concave function of x.

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:30.080
Actually, this is
already super interesting

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:37.610
because C could be the set
of values, where some product

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:39.470
actually works or functions.

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:41.510
It meets all the specs.

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:47.720
Then y actually tells you
about the distribution

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:50.720
under manufacture of
those specifications.

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.300
Then x is like the target.

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:57.170
It's how you actually, in
the factory, what you target.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:02.870
So I will target 3 nanometers
for this critical dimension,

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:04.790
for example.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:08.990
And then what this tells you is
if you're targeting this and y

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:11.247
gives you your
manufacturing errors--

00:13:11.247 --> 00:13:12.830
I asked for 3
nanometers, I'm actually

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:16.160
going to get let's
say, I don't know,

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:19.850
some distribution
that's a log-concave,

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:21.560
it'll be spread around that.

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:25.490
And what this says, this then
becomes basically the yield.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:27.450
And we'll talk about that.

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.413
So it says that the
yield is log-concave.

00:13:30.413 --> 00:13:31.330
We'll talk about that.

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:34.350
But that's already getting
very close to a very serious

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.720
application.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.290
Now how do you show this?

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:42.510
Well, I'm just going to write
it as an integral formula.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:48.450
And what I do is I have
two things here. p, that's

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:50.160
my assumption log-concave.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:52.470
And I have this
function g of x plus y.

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:55.290
And g is a 0, 1
indicator function.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:59.010
It's 1, if you're in C,
0, if you're outside.

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:02.250
That's a log-concave
function because it's

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:04.350
a log-concave function
that basically has

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:07.110
value 0 inside the log of that.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.600
It has the log of the
indicator is 0 inside C,

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:13.560
and it's minus infinity outside
C. That's log-concave function.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:21.390
And if you work out what
this integral is, it's that.

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:25.820
Oh, and here, this
thing is actually

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:28.950
a log-concave
function precomposed

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:30.330
with an affine mapping.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:33.990
And that is going to
be also log-concave.

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.687
So there you go.

00:14:35.687 --> 00:14:36.520
Something like that.

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:43.820
And let's talk about this yield
function a little bit more

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:45.990
just for fun.

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.810
So here you go.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:53.490
We have x are some nominal
parameter values for a product.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:57.150
It's what you intend
to manufacture.

00:14:57.150 --> 00:15:00.420
And this is a vector
of different things.

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:03.584
S is going to be the set
of acceptable values.

00:15:03.584 --> 00:15:07.140
So if you're
designing a circuit,

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:12.330
it would tell you if
various parameters, based

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:14.250
on your circuit, come
out in the right range,

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:16.080
it's actually going to work.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.990
So that's an example.

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:22.290
And then w.

00:15:22.290 --> 00:15:23.940
This is what we target.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:28.020
And w is this model's
random variations

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.030
that happens under manufacture.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.310
So that's it.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:33.690
And then this is the yield.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:39.480
And it's a yield as a function
of basically the settings

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:41.250
you target.

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:43.920
And so, it's pretty
clear what you want.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:49.180
If I wanted large yield
and I had a set here,

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:51.430
I mean, the intuition
behind this is pretty clear.

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:52.880
Here, here's my set.

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:57.640
So it works if
you land in there.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.490
And let's make it simple, let's
assume that the perturbations

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:01.780
are Gaussian.

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:05.470
So what happens is you
tell me what the yield is.

00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:07.360
What have I set my targets here?

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:08.950
And here's Gaussian.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:11.710
There's one sigma two sigma.

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:15.620
Go ahead, what's
the yield for that?

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:17.228
[INAUDIBLE]

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:23.140
I don't know, 10%?

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:24.510
Which is not very good.

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:25.450
Everybody got that?

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:26.775
So make sense?

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:32.160
So that was a poor choice of x.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:35.760
What if I'm right
here, and let's say

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:37.260
that's the one sigma thing.

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:40.200
What's the yield
if I target there?

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.100
What is it?

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:42.300
50.

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:44.130
Yeah, it's a little
bit less than 50.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:49.020
And if this was just a
half-space, it'd be 50%.

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.200
But also fail by
coming out over here.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:53.730
So let's say it's 45%.

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.630
I'm making that
up, but that's it.

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:57.180
Everybody got this?

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:59.610
Now after these
examples, you tell me

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:03.820
what should I target
to maximize the yield?

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:05.530
I mean, it's kind of obvious.

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:06.099
The middle.

00:17:06.099 --> 00:17:07.540
Yeah, the center.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:12.550
Yeah, so what I should
do is set my target here.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:14.829
And then if that's
1 sigma and 2 sigma,

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:18.160
I'm going to have a
very happy yield there.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:20.980
Going to be 90% or 95%
or something like that.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:22.599
Everybody got this?

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:24.099
So this is in 2D.

00:17:24.099 --> 00:17:26.140
And what this says is
this goes down in 10.

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:29.020
The exact same
problem is you could

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:32.650
do this in 10 dimensions
or something like that.

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:36.310
By the way, you said
the center of the set.

00:17:36.310 --> 00:17:38.080
And what's interesting
is that's something

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:39.950
we're going to look
at later in the class.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:42.490
But turns out there's a ton
of different definitions

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:46.990
of the center of a set,
which we'll get to later.

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:49.000
Actually, we may
see one later today.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:50.770
It depends how far I
get, but we'll see.

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:51.790
We may see one later.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:55.000
So in R it's very simple.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:57.700
If I give you an
interval, there's

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:01.970
not much discussion about what
the center of an interval is.

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:03.650
It's the midpoint
of the interval.

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.290
Any sensible center
is going to be that.

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:09.320
But immediately when you
get into two dimensions,

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.480
it's not clear what
the center of a set is.

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:15.110
And we'll see there's lots of
different definitions of it.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:20.220
So what this says is
actually super interesting.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:22.910
It says that y is log-concave.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:26.690
And that says that if
I fix a minimum yield,

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:29.900
we cannot economically
manufacture this product unless

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:32.360
the yield is bigger than 85%.

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:36.820
Then it says this is a yield
region, and it's convex.

00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:39.020
It could be empty, which
would be too bad for you,

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:41.300
but it is convex.

00:18:41.300 --> 00:18:44.900
And the reason is
this is log-concave.

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:48.350
So if I exponentiate
both sides or take a log,

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:50.510
I get log y is bigger
than log alpha.

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:52.310
Log y is concave.

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:53.780
This is a super-level set.

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:56.345
And a super-level set of a
concave function is convex.

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.460
Everybody following this?

00:19:01.460 --> 00:19:07.040
So this is actually close to
an actual application that's

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:11.850
incredibly useful,
which should be obvious.

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.340
And the last generalization
we're going to look at

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:17.650
is convexity with respect
to a generalized inequality.

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:20.580
So here it is.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:24.420
Here I have a function that
maps Rn not into R but into Rm,

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:25.770
so it's vector-valued.

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:28.425
So you give it x, and
it produces a vector.

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:34.350
We say that this
function is K-convex.

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:37.530
K is a proper cone.

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:40.817
And we say that that's true
if Jensen's inequality holds,

00:19:40.817 --> 00:19:42.900
except that the left- and
right-hand sides are all

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:44.880
vectors.

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:47.310
And the inequality
is the inequality

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:49.500
induced by this cone.

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:53.460
So most famous example is
with symmetric matrices.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:55.830
So if I have a mapping
from symmetric matrices

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:58.390
to symmetric matrices--
here's an example.

00:19:58.390 --> 00:19:59.850
f of capital X is x squared.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.470
X is a symmetric matrix.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:05.610
And the function
is, I square it.

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:09.900
By the way, you'd guess it
sure sounds convex, right?

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:15.250
Because it's the square, and
you know that in a scalar case,

00:20:15.250 --> 00:20:15.750
x is.

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:19.830
And in this case, that
guess turns out to be true.

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:22.360
What you'd have to
show is the following.

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:24.240
It says, what you
want to show is if I

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:28.450
take two symmetric matrices.

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:31.150
If I then form a
mixture of them,

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:34.420
so theta x plus 1 minus theta y.

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:35.680
That's a new symmetric matrix.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:40.810
If I square that, that's
less than or equal to theta

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:44.660
x squared plus theta 1
minus theta y squared.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:45.710
What does that mean?

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:50.042
That means that this inequality
here holds for all z.

00:20:50.042 --> 00:20:52.840
So this is what this means.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.440
We could look at this.

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:03.310
This does hold for all z
because these are all simply

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:04.780
convex functions.

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:10.870
And this thing is a
convex function here of x.

00:21:10.870 --> 00:21:17.740
So that requires a tricky
reading there, but it is.

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:19.960
So this is the idea.

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:23.680
Like with all generalizations,
you want to be on your toes

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:27.830
because not everything
you think is true.

00:21:27.830 --> 00:21:31.580
I think a famous one
is the exponential

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:33.260
of a symmetric matrix.

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:35.300
That sure sounds convex.

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:36.890
I think it's not.

00:21:36.890 --> 00:21:38.600
I could be wrong on that.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:40.850
That just came out,
and maybe I'm wrong.

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:43.010
But if it's not
x, it's something

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:47.480
like that, where it sure sounds
like it ought to be true.

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:49.940
And it just turns out, it's not.

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:51.380
That one might be actually.

00:21:51.380 --> 00:21:53.450
I can't remember what is
the simple example that

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:55.610
violates that.

00:21:55.610 --> 00:22:01.880
So that wraps up, actually,
all the analysis you

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.640
need to know for the course.

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:10.310
What I would actually recommend
doing is every now and then,

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:13.310
give yourself a half
an hour and go back

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:17.240
to chapters either 1, 2, or
3, and just reread part of it.

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:18.950
What's going to
happen is something

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:22.130
that made absolutely
no sense whatsoever

00:22:22.130 --> 00:22:25.890
to you maybe two weeks ago is
going to start making sense.

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:28.807
I mean, for one thing, it'll be
the fourth time you've seen it

00:22:28.807 --> 00:22:30.390
or something like
that, but also, it's

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:31.750
going to connect to other stuff.

00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:35.400
So this would be my
strong recommendation

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:38.340
to do something like that.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:41.290
So now we're
starting on problems,

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:44.520
which is not analysis.

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:47.580
And, of course, the underlying
point of all of this

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:50.130
is the following.

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:53.820
Convex optimization
problems are tractable.

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:55.980
Basically, we can solve them.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.480
That's the point.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:01.830
So it's not a question
of math when you say, oh,

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:03.870
that problem is convex.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.820
I mean, sure it
allows you to make

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.580
certain assertions about here
are the optimality conditions.

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.560
I mean, whatever.

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:13.530
The most important part about
it is you can actually solve it.

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:17.160
Now that will segue to in
maybe three or four weeks

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:18.250
or something like that.

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:21.060
But that's what makes
everything you just

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:24.120
did in the last two
weeks actionable,

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:27.107
so that's going to be the point.

00:23:27.107 --> 00:23:28.690
So what we're going
to do now is we're

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:31.870
going to march through
what convex optimization

00:23:31.870 --> 00:23:33.190
problems are.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:35.380
I mean, we already introduced
this very informally

00:23:35.380 --> 00:23:39.010
on day one, but now we're
going to do it more carefully.

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:41.230
So first of all,
let's talk about what

00:23:41.230 --> 00:23:43.930
is an optimization problem.

00:23:43.930 --> 00:23:49.270
So I actually think of it
as an object, literally,

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:53.530
in the computer science
sense, it has attributes.

00:23:53.530 --> 00:23:59.110
It has an attribute, which is,
say, this is the objective.

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:04.690
And the objective is to minimize
f0 of x. f0 of x is a function.

00:24:04.690 --> 00:24:09.850
So by the way, minimize is
not a keyword in mathematics.

00:24:09.850 --> 00:24:11.050
It is not.

00:24:11.050 --> 00:24:14.470
Min is, and it only
means and can only

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:18.070
mean one thing, which is
the minimum of a finite set

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:19.330
of numbers, period.

00:24:19.330 --> 00:24:22.510
That's the only definition
of min that's in math.

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:24.070
Min and minimize
are not the same.

00:24:26.245 --> 00:24:28.120
When I get lazy and I
don't want to write out

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:29.860
minimize on the
board, I'll write min,

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:31.990
but I'll put a period
there at the end.

00:24:35.530 --> 00:24:41.890
If I don't, then you should
call me on it or something.

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:44.920
So we minimize an
objective function here.

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:45.610
Subject to.

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:47.860
And we have inequality
constraints and equality

00:24:47.860 --> 00:24:49.240
constraints.

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:52.280
Now for simplicity, we're going
to call this the standard form.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:55.420
In the standard form,
you're minimizing something.

00:24:55.420 --> 00:24:57.128
You could easily
maximize something.

00:24:57.128 --> 00:24:59.170
And there's whole fields
where everything they do

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:02.980
is they maximize, like in
economics, you maximize utility

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:06.250
or you maximize profit
or something like that.

00:25:06.250 --> 00:25:07.840
So there's different fields.

00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:11.440
And then all you have to
do is minimize minus f0.

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:12.250
There's a question.

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:14.985
Yeah, does the inequality
always have to be less than

00:25:14.985 --> 00:25:16.360
or equal like an
upper bound or--

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:18.050
Yeah, so that's
a great question.

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:20.360
This is just to make
it standard form.

00:25:20.360 --> 00:25:23.680
This question is, does it
always have to be less than

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:24.250
or equal to?

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:25.990
It can be bigger
than or equal to.

00:25:25.990 --> 00:25:30.460
Suppose you wanted to impose
the constraint that f1 of x

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:32.150
is bigger than or equal to 0.

00:25:32.150 --> 00:25:35.690
What you'd do is you'd
write that as minus f of x

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:37.685
is less than or equal to 0.

00:25:40.412 --> 00:25:41.870
And we're going to
be talking a lot

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:43.287
about these little
transformations

00:25:43.287 --> 00:25:46.142
that you can do to map
things into this form.

00:25:46.142 --> 00:25:47.600
Actually, a whole
bunch of them are

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:52.160
going to be done automatically
for you in some cases.

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.170
And we have a
equality constraints.

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:59.290
And so, this is the idea.

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:03.200
The optimal value
of this problem--

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:04.520
so this is an object.

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:05.955
It's a problem.

00:26:05.955 --> 00:26:08.080
It's got a bunch of different
fields or attributes.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:09.850
It's got it's objective.

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:12.160
It's got a list of
inequality constraints.

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:13.990
It's got a list of
equality constraints.

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:20.300
So that's the way I think
of an optimization problem.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:26.300
So its optimal value
is the following.

00:26:26.300 --> 00:26:29.800
It is inf is
essentially the minimum.

00:26:29.800 --> 00:26:33.790
It's the minimum of the
objective value over all points

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:35.320
x that are feasible.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:36.850
And to be feasible
means you have

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:40.330
to satisfy these inequalities
and all of these equalities

00:26:40.330 --> 00:26:41.230
like that.

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:46.270
So this predicate here
says x is feasible.

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:49.690
And it says if among
all such x's, you

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:52.990
have the smallest
value of the objective,

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:57.750
then that value is
the optimal value.

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:00.208
And that's denoted p star.

00:27:00.208 --> 00:27:02.000
By the way, there's a
slight subtlety here.

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:05.330
That's a star not an asterisk.

00:27:05.330 --> 00:27:08.210
Asterisk is reserved for
things like conjugates

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:11.660
and some other stuff that we'll
be doing in a couple of weeks.

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:14.165
But here, star means optimal.

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:19.010
Now this is standard math.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:21.890
If this set is empty,
that means there

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:24.710
is no x that satisfies these.

00:27:24.710 --> 00:27:29.000
Then, by definition, the minimum
or the infimum of the empty set

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:30.980
is plus infinity.

00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:32.660
Actually, it's not
a bad definition.

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:33.660
Well, it doesn't matter.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:36.710
It is the definition.

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:39.260
And everything makes sense then.

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:42.673
A bunch of rules you'd
imagine work with that.

00:27:42.673 --> 00:27:44.090
But that's the
standard definition

00:27:44.090 --> 00:27:46.970
that we-- so if I say p star is
infinity for this problem, what

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:52.610
it means is there is no x
that satisfies the inequality.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:54.350
Now there's another
thing that can

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:56.533
happen is you can have p
star equals minus infinity.

00:27:56.533 --> 00:27:57.950
And I'll tell you
what that means.

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:01.160
What it means is there's a
sequence of points every.

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:03.060
Single one of them is feasible.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.570
And this objective goes
down and down and down.

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:09.140
It gets as small as you like.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:11.600
Both of these, by the
way, are pathologies

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:14.360
in a practical sense
of the problem.

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:16.160
So if I walk up
to you and say, I

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:19.640
need you to solve this
problem and it's infeasible,

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:20.900
what does that mean?

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:24.193
If you say, I want to
construct the optimal schedule

00:28:24.193 --> 00:28:26.360
or something for something
in the optimal allocation

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:29.540
of resources, and it's
infeasible, I mean,

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:31.430
as a practical matter,
it's of no use.

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:32.990
I mean, actually,
all it really tells

00:28:32.990 --> 00:28:36.860
you is that formulated my
problem in a stupid way.

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:40.040
This is called unbounded below.

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:43.438
And that's also a
pathology typically.

00:28:43.438 --> 00:28:45.980
Actually, there's a wonderful
name for this in some contexts.

00:28:45.980 --> 00:28:49.040
In some contexts, in
risk-averse control,

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:54.110
this thing is referred to as
euphoric breakdown, which is,

00:28:54.110 --> 00:28:57.395
I think, a great phrase for it.

00:28:57.395 --> 00:28:59.270
Let's remember what the
semantics of this is.

00:28:59.270 --> 00:29:03.590
The semantics is we
are OK with any x that

00:29:03.590 --> 00:29:05.150
satisfies these constraints.

00:29:05.150 --> 00:29:09.050
And we are completely not OK
with anything that violates

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:11.540
them even slightly, number one.

00:29:11.540 --> 00:29:15.350
Then among ones that are
OK, which means feasible,

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:20.750
then we are happier
the smaller f0 gets.

00:29:20.750 --> 00:29:24.410
So if, in fact, there are
feasible points, with values,

00:29:24.410 --> 00:29:28.320
it's basically, hey,
minimize my cost,

00:29:28.320 --> 00:29:29.630
which is my negative profit.

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:31.610
And you go, yeah,
well, I mean, here's

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:36.020
a feasible x that
gives u minus 10.

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:38.573
And you go cool, I
just made $10 million.

00:29:38.573 --> 00:29:40.490
And they go, here's
another one with minus 20.

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:43.610
And you go $20
million, that's great.

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:46.520
Anyway, so that's
why that's called

00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:49.580
sometimes euphoric breakdown.

00:29:49.580 --> 00:29:54.330
So this is just
the nomenclature.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:58.320
So we can talk about
what is a point.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:29:59.860
Is it optimal and all that.

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:01.890
But first, we say that
a point is feasible

00:30:01.890 --> 00:30:04.050
if it's in the domain
of the objective

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:06.030
and it satisfies
the constraints.

00:30:06.030 --> 00:30:11.730
And we say it's optimal if
it simply is feasible and has

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:13.830
this optimal value.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:19.020
And then capital X opt, that's
the set of optimal points

00:30:19.020 --> 00:30:21.300
which, by the way, is convex.

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:24.810
It's not hard to show.

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:32.850
Because it's the p star
sublevel sets of f0

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:35.860
intersect the feasible
set which is convex.

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:37.290
So if you follow that, great.

00:30:37.290 --> 00:30:40.620
If you didn't, that's OK, too.

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:42.960
Now we have another concept
which is locally optimal.

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:47.580
And locally optimal,
very roughly speaking,

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:51.510
it says that you're optimal
in a restricted range

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:53.800
around that current point.

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:55.870
And, here, I'll draw one.

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:58.270
It's our last example here.

00:30:58.270 --> 00:31:00.310
I'll just draw that function.

00:31:00.310 --> 00:31:05.780
I'll try to get a marker
that's actually visible.

00:31:05.780 --> 00:31:08.560
So we have x cubed minus 3x.

00:31:08.560 --> 00:31:12.650
And that function
looks like this.

00:31:12.650 --> 00:31:15.590
That's minus 1
and that's plus 1.

00:31:15.590 --> 00:31:18.440
That's your function.

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:20.150
That's x cubed minus 3x.

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:25.760
And so, what's p
star for that one?

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:27.110
No constraints.

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:29.450
Just what is p star?

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:33.320
It's minus infinity
because these values

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:36.740
go as low as you like.

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:42.350
Then this is a
locally optimal point.

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:47.090
Because, for example,
if I add the constraint

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:50.270
that x is between 0.5 and 1.5.

00:31:50.270 --> 00:31:52.790
If it's within 0.5 of 1.

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:56.030
And then I simply ask,
what's the minimum?

00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:57.500
That's a locally optimal point.

00:31:57.500 --> 00:31:59.690
It's optimal for that
restricted problem.

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:02.600
So that's locally optimal.

00:32:02.600 --> 00:32:06.170
Let's look at a couple of other
examples just to explain this.

00:32:06.170 --> 00:32:09.590
Let's take f of x is 1 over x.

00:32:09.590 --> 00:32:12.920
The domain is R plus
plus, so positive things.

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:14.900
It just goes down like this.

00:32:14.900 --> 00:32:20.482
And p star is 0 because
the infimum of 1 over x.

00:32:20.482 --> 00:32:22.940
As x gets bigger and bigger,
that gets smaller and smaller.

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:24.110
It's going towards 0.

00:32:24.110 --> 00:32:26.610
So p star is 0.

00:32:26.610 --> 00:32:28.600
There's no optimal point.

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:30.600
And you're not allowed
to say x equals infinity.

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:33.040
That's not a valid choice.

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:35.010
So there's no optimal point.

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:38.190
There's no x for which
1 over x is equal to 0.

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:44.580
How about minus log x?

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:47.220
That's convex.

00:32:47.220 --> 00:32:50.100
The domain is,
again, R plus plus.

00:32:50.100 --> 00:32:55.110
And that's actually
unbounded below.

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:58.620
Just like here, it's got
something that goes down to 0.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:04.890
So here, as x gets really
big, this gets as negative

00:33:04.890 --> 00:33:06.510
as you would like.

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:08.160
Maybe slowly, doesn't matter.

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:11.310
It's p star is minus infinity.

00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:12.150
x log x.

00:33:12.150 --> 00:33:13.950
That's the negative entropy.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:16.080
And that looks like this.

00:33:19.470 --> 00:33:23.110
That's this function here.

00:33:23.110 --> 00:33:24.430
Looks like that, roughly.

00:33:24.430 --> 00:33:26.710
Oh, it's got an
infinite slope here,

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:28.420
so I'll make it like that.

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:30.220
It's got infinite
slope right here.

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:34.420
By the way, you can either
define the value at 0

00:33:34.420 --> 00:33:40.150
to be 0, which is what it
would be by continuity.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:44.290
Where the expression x log x
doesn't quite make sense there.

00:33:44.290 --> 00:33:48.730
I mean, just by continuity,
you could make it 0 there.

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:50.200
And that's what people do.

00:33:52.940 --> 00:33:57.850
This one has no pathologies.

00:33:57.850 --> 00:34:00.460
It's got a single
minimum right there.

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:04.570
And that value is at 1 over e.

00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:05.500
There you go.

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:09.219
That's the minimum value
of the negative entropy

00:34:09.219 --> 00:34:11.680
or that maximizes
this entropy function.

00:34:14.290 --> 00:34:18.304
So this is just to give you the
nomenclature of optimization

00:34:18.304 --> 00:34:18.804
problems.

00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:22.290
A few more things.

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:22.790
Yeah.

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:27.679
So why is there--

00:34:27.679 --> 00:34:30.350
in the last example,
you can say that there's

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:33.739
a fourth break down the
p star negative infinity.

00:34:33.739 --> 00:34:37.310
But then the first example,
you can't say the same?

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:40.100
Because this is not
unbounded below.

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:43.130
Unbounded below means
that the objective values

00:34:43.130 --> 00:34:45.170
get as negative as you like.

00:34:45.170 --> 00:34:51.575
These things, they go down,
but they only go down to 0.

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:57.890
Euphoria kicks in at around
minus 10, in my experience.

00:34:57.890 --> 00:35:01.430
Minus 100 feels great, but
I'm just joking, of course.

00:35:04.390 --> 00:35:05.200
Yeah.

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:08.650
The first example is feasible,
it just has no optimum?

00:35:08.650 --> 00:35:09.860
Yeah, the first example.

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:15.977
All of these problems
are feasible,

00:35:15.977 --> 00:35:17.560
and we could write
down an infeasible.

00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:19.973
Why is the second one
considered feasible?

00:35:19.973 --> 00:35:21.545
Even if the time had gone up.

00:35:21.545 --> 00:35:22.260
Oh, this one.

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:30.590
Oh, well, those two pathologies
are at the opposite.

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:33.260
Infeasible means
there's no exit even

00:35:33.260 --> 00:35:35.390
satisfies your constraints,
so let's not even

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:37.970
talk about the objective.

00:35:37.970 --> 00:35:39.380
That's infeasible.

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:43.010
Unbounded below is, oh,
there's a lot of x's that

00:35:43.010 --> 00:35:44.240
satisfy your constraints.

00:35:44.240 --> 00:35:48.740
Not only that, among them, f0
can be as negative as you like.

00:35:48.740 --> 00:35:52.550
So, actually, they
are both pathologies,

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:55.542
but to me, they're
at the opposite ends.

00:35:55.542 --> 00:35:56.750
They're the different things.

00:35:56.750 --> 00:35:57.320
Yes.

00:35:57.320 --> 00:36:00.765
Is there a term for the
first x that reaches 0?

00:36:03.510 --> 00:36:05.628
By reaches 0--

00:36:05.628 --> 00:36:06.870
[INAUDIBLE]

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:07.790
What's that?

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:10.050
I guess they never
do actually reach 0.

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:10.780
You mean up here?

00:36:10.780 --> 00:36:11.280
Yeah.

00:36:11.280 --> 00:36:12.070
For example.

00:36:12.070 --> 00:36:13.590
Yes.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:14.880
[INAUDIBLE]

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:19.660
Yeah, let's do another example.

00:36:19.660 --> 00:36:22.290
Let me add a
constraint to this one.

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:29.550
I'll add the constraint that
x is less than or equal to 3.

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:31.290
Sorry, I will write this way.

00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:33.150
There we go.

00:36:33.150 --> 00:36:35.280
I think that was your
question about weather--

00:36:35.280 --> 00:36:38.520
yeah, so I'll write x
minus 3 less than 0.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:40.770
So I'm going to add that.

00:36:40.770 --> 00:36:43.953
I want you to minimize--

00:36:43.953 --> 00:36:45.370
here, watch, I'm
going to be good.

00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:47.790
I'm going to write minimize out.

00:36:47.790 --> 00:36:50.940
Minimize 1 over x.

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:54.090
Then this is subject to this.

00:36:54.090 --> 00:36:57.480
Oh, by the way,
for us this thing

00:36:57.480 --> 00:37:02.475
has a domain, which is equal
to R all positive numbers.

00:37:06.300 --> 00:37:08.940
Let's talk about that problem.

00:37:08.940 --> 00:37:11.340
Is it feasible?

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:12.200
It is.

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:16.640
There are points which are
in the domain of f0 which

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:17.810
are less than or equal to 3.

00:37:17.810 --> 00:37:18.620
Here's a good example.

00:37:18.620 --> 00:37:19.120
Ready?

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:22.340
1, x equals 1.

00:37:22.340 --> 00:37:25.730
So it's feasible.

00:37:25.730 --> 00:37:28.960
What's its optimal value?

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:29.710
1/3

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:30.790
It's 1/3.

00:37:30.790 --> 00:37:36.010
Does it have an optimal point
or multiple optimal points?

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:38.230
Name me an optimal point.

00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:39.250
x equals 3.

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:40.270
x equals 3.

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:41.570
Are there any others?

00:37:41.570 --> 00:37:42.070
No.

00:37:42.070 --> 00:37:43.480
No, right, exactly.

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:48.190
So in this case, this
set x opt is a Singleton.

00:37:48.190 --> 00:37:50.500
And you would say it has
a unique solution, which

00:37:50.500 --> 00:37:53.380
is x equals 3.

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:54.760
That's a well-posed problem.

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:58.120
I mean, it's a silly one, but
it's a well-posed problem.

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:07.980
So there's a concept of implicit
versus explicit constraints.

00:38:07.980 --> 00:38:09.480
And these are
actually going to be

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:12.400
rather important in practice.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:14.790
So here's what they are.

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:17.940
So when I have a
standard form problem,

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.310
I've got my objective
function f0.

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:23.160
I've got my inequality
constraints fi.

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:28.200
And I have my equality
constraint functions hi.

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:30.960
So the first thing
is we can't even

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:33.870
talk about an x if it's
not in the domain of all

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:34.980
those functions.

00:38:34.980 --> 00:38:37.710
It just doesn't even make sense.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:42.260
This is, by the way, worse
than being infeasible.

00:38:42.260 --> 00:38:44.690
Infeasible is like,
hey, try this.

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:50.180
And you go, sorry, that doesn't
satisfy the third inequality.

00:38:50.180 --> 00:38:51.380
That's infeasible.

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:57.110
But this is, thanks
for proposing an x.

00:38:57.110 --> 00:39:00.890
I can't even evaluate
the objective on it.

00:39:00.890 --> 00:39:03.530
Or I can't even evaluate
the third constraint.

00:39:06.920 --> 00:39:09.320
Yeah, a lot of people
don't distinguish

00:39:09.320 --> 00:39:12.950
between being out of domain
and being plus infinity.

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:15.020
We'll get to that,
but it's fine.

00:39:15.020 --> 00:39:19.880
So this is referred to as
an implicit constraint.

00:39:23.480 --> 00:39:25.190
Here's an example here.

00:39:25.190 --> 00:39:29.000
There's an implicit
constraint that x is positive.

00:39:29.000 --> 00:39:31.840
The reason is, this has a
domain, which is R plus plus.

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:36.550
Simply writing it down.

00:39:36.550 --> 00:39:40.840
You write down log of z, and
there's an implicit constraint

00:39:40.840 --> 00:39:44.920
that z is positive, period.

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:46.220
I mean, in this class.

00:39:46.220 --> 00:39:48.640
If you're taking a class
on complex analysis,

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:49.840
z could be negative.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:53.620
But the social
contract in this class,

00:39:53.620 --> 00:39:57.280
where we focus on
convexity, is log

00:39:57.280 --> 00:39:59.200
has a domain of R plus plus.

00:39:59.200 --> 00:40:02.620
Same for 1 over x, by the way.

00:40:02.620 --> 00:40:05.710
So these are implicit.

00:40:05.710 --> 00:40:08.320
And this is called the
domain of the problem.

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:11.530
And so, if you propose an
x, it's outside the domain,

00:40:11.530 --> 00:40:13.420
it just doesn't even make sense.

00:40:13.420 --> 00:40:18.280
I mean, not only should
whoever is evaluating the x

00:40:18.280 --> 00:40:19.318
give you a--

00:40:19.318 --> 00:40:21.610
they should give you a really
nasty message back, which

00:40:21.610 --> 00:40:25.210
is like your choice
was so bad, I

00:40:25.210 --> 00:40:28.910
couldn't even evaluate the
objective or constraints

00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:29.600
to see.

00:40:29.600 --> 00:40:30.530
So

00:40:30.530 --> 00:40:32.520
And, basically, you should be
put on warning at that point.

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:34.353
And if you do it two
more times, you're out.

00:40:34.353 --> 00:40:35.450
You're barred from it.

00:40:35.450 --> 00:40:37.912
It would be as if the
problem if the variable was

00:40:37.912 --> 00:40:40.370
like a positive definite matrix
that represents, let's say,

00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:41.810
a covariance matrix.

00:40:41.810 --> 00:40:44.480
And you go, well,
how about this?

00:40:44.480 --> 00:40:46.940
And I give you a
non-symmetric matrix

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:49.440
with negative and
complex eigenvalues,

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:51.320
then you're like, hello?

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:52.730
Like, what?

00:40:52.730 --> 00:40:55.748
I can't evaluate the
log likelihood of that

00:40:55.748 --> 00:40:57.290
because it's not a
covariance matrix.

00:40:57.290 --> 00:40:58.373
It doesn't make any sense.

00:40:58.373 --> 00:41:02.130
Anyway, that's what this is.

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:04.950
Now we'll say a problem is
unconstrained if you just have

00:41:04.950 --> 00:41:07.260
no inequalities or whatever.

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.600
So here's an example.

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:12.970
This is an
unconstrained problem.

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:15.190
The reason is there
is a constraint, which

00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:16.870
is ai transpose
x is less than b,

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:18.730
but that's built into the log.

00:41:18.730 --> 00:41:21.430
Because in this course, you
take the log of something,

00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:23.140
it's positive, period.

00:41:23.140 --> 00:41:25.330
We don't even
define it otherwise.

00:41:25.330 --> 00:41:26.785
So that's what that is.

00:41:26.785 --> 00:41:27.535
That's an example.

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:33.230
Here's another one
we'll talk about later.

00:41:33.230 --> 00:41:35.180
It's called a
feasibility problem.

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:36.680
Sometimes, people
write it this way.

00:41:36.680 --> 00:41:38.660
It says, find an
x that satisfies

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:41.510
a bunch of constraints.

00:41:41.510 --> 00:41:44.420
Oh, by the way, some people call
constraints, hard constraints

00:41:44.420 --> 00:41:48.020
because, basically,
they are utterly

00:41:48.020 --> 00:41:50.060
and completely non-negotiable.

00:41:53.270 --> 00:41:55.670
If you satisfy
them, we don't care

00:41:55.670 --> 00:41:58.280
if you satisfy them just
barely or by a big margin,

00:41:58.280 --> 00:42:00.080
we don't care.

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:01.858
So those are called
hard constraints.

00:42:01.858 --> 00:42:03.650
And so, this is just
a feasibility problem.

00:42:03.650 --> 00:42:06.380
And it says, yeah,
please, can you

00:42:06.380 --> 00:42:08.150
find an x that satisfies this?

00:42:10.910 --> 00:42:15.110
And I think some people
have a whole quasitheory

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:16.580
of optimization problems.

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:18.770
And they refer to
this as, I think,

00:42:18.770 --> 00:42:20.780
one name is a
satisficing problem.

00:42:20.780 --> 00:42:23.360
I mean, frankly, I find
it profoundly stupid.

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:25.880
And if you understand
any of this stuff,

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:28.460
it's completely standard and
straightforward how to solve.

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:29.560
Anyway, fine.

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:32.460
So that's a feasibility problem.

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:38.940
Then you can write
it as a special case

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:40.050
of our general problem.

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:42.610
What you do is you introduce
an objective function,

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:46.180
which is just 0, no matter
what, and has domain everything.

00:42:46.180 --> 00:42:49.050
This is really dumb,
but it does the trick.

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:51.360
So if I say minimize 0.

00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:55.440
What I'm really saying
is, I have 0 affect.

00:42:55.440 --> 00:42:57.660
At least on the
objective, I don't care.

00:42:57.660 --> 00:43:02.460
Any x you propose, as far as
the objective is concerned,

00:43:02.460 --> 00:43:03.790
they're all the same.

00:43:03.790 --> 00:43:06.540
And so, here you
have the constraints.

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:09.660
And so, for this
problem, p star is 0

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:11.688
if there is a feasible point.

00:43:11.688 --> 00:43:13.230
If there's a point
that satisfies it,

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:17.850
and then that actually
means it's optimal, too.

00:43:17.850 --> 00:43:20.860
And p star is just infinity if
the constraints are infeasible.

00:43:20.860 --> 00:43:24.870
So so-called
feasibility problems

00:43:24.870 --> 00:43:27.460
are the same as our
master problems.

00:43:27.460 --> 00:43:28.860
They're just the same thing.

00:43:28.860 --> 00:43:30.152
I mean, they're kind of boring.

00:43:30.152 --> 00:43:35.940
They only take the value
0 or infinity Makes sense?

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:37.140
What--

00:43:37.140 --> 00:43:40.900
And, by the way, this comes
up in a whole lot of things.

00:43:40.900 --> 00:43:44.450
It will come up, for
example, in economics.

00:43:47.145 --> 00:43:50.190
This would be the
absence of arbitrage.

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:52.350
And so, that'll work this way.

00:43:52.350 --> 00:43:55.470
x is something you
can do, like buy

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:57.870
a bunch of these instruments
and some bonds over there

00:43:57.870 --> 00:44:00.180
and some derivatives
and some other things.

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:02.430
That some portfolio
you would construct.

00:44:02.430 --> 00:44:09.000
And then what these state is
that no matter what happens,

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:11.190
you will actually
not lose money.

00:44:11.190 --> 00:44:13.840
And, at least one case,
you'll make money.

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:15.270
That's a feasibility problem.

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:18.420
And then if it's
feasible, you would

00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:22.752
say there's arbitrage
in that market.

00:44:22.752 --> 00:44:24.210
We'll get to that
later, but I just

00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:27.510
thought I'd just mention that
feasibility problems that

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:29.940
are literally
feasibility problems,

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:31.860
they're going to come
up in real applications.

00:44:31.860 --> 00:44:32.710
That--

00:44:32.710 --> 00:44:35.258
Just to clarify, they're both
the exact same thing, right?

00:44:35.258 --> 00:44:36.300
I'm sorry, I missed that.

00:44:36.300 --> 00:44:36.870
What was that?

00:44:36.870 --> 00:44:38.835
Just to clarify, they're both
saying the exact same thing,

00:44:38.835 --> 00:44:39.030
right?

00:44:39.030 --> 00:44:40.655
They are saying
exactly the same thing.

00:44:40.655 --> 00:44:42.240
Some people write it this way.

00:44:42.240 --> 00:44:45.858
I mean, if you were
talking to a normal person,

00:44:45.858 --> 00:44:46.650
you might say that.

00:44:46.650 --> 00:44:51.630
I mean, this just maps it
into our standard form.

00:44:51.630 --> 00:44:54.000
Is there another question
somewhere over there?

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:54.930
OK.

00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:57.060
All right, so that's
a feasibility problem.

00:44:57.060 --> 00:45:01.920
And, finally, now we get to a
convex optimization problem.

00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:04.735
And here it is.

00:45:04.735 --> 00:45:07.380
It is this.

00:45:07.380 --> 00:45:11.130
It's a standard form
problem where we restrict

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:13.350
the curvature of the functions.

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:14.800
That's all it is.

00:45:14.800 --> 00:45:15.870
And it's this.

00:45:15.870 --> 00:45:20.160
The objective that we
minimize has to be convex.

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:25.900
The constraints which we are, in
this case, upper bounding by 0

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:28.510
have to be convex functions.

00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:31.540
And the equality
constraints have

00:45:31.540 --> 00:45:34.900
to be affine or what people
call them linear equality

00:45:34.900 --> 00:45:35.780
constraints.

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:37.280
So these are the rules.

00:45:37.280 --> 00:45:40.240
So roughly speaking,
it says objective

00:45:40.240 --> 00:45:41.800
has non-negative curvature.

00:45:41.800 --> 00:45:45.070
Constraints all have
non-negative curvature.

00:45:45.070 --> 00:45:49.790
And equality constraints
all have zero curvature,

00:45:49.790 --> 00:45:51.250
which is affine.

00:45:51.250 --> 00:45:51.970
Makes sense?

00:45:51.970 --> 00:45:52.540
Yeah.

00:45:52.540 --> 00:45:54.520
Why do the quality
constraints need

00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:58.300
to be affine and not convex?

00:45:58.300 --> 00:45:59.080
Why?

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:01.720
That's a loaded
question because this

00:46:01.720 --> 00:46:06.080
is the definition of a
convex optimization problem.

00:46:06.080 --> 00:46:09.310
So morally speaking, I think
I can answer it this way.

00:46:09.310 --> 00:46:13.120
I'm allowed to say because
that's the definition.

00:46:13.120 --> 00:46:15.280
But, I mean, that's
kind of obnoxious.

00:46:15.280 --> 00:46:23.530
But we'll get to some
interesting examples

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:26.530
when you have an equality
constraint that's not.

00:46:26.530 --> 00:46:27.310
Let's see.

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:30.110
Well, by definition, you
don't have a convex problem.

00:46:30.110 --> 00:46:33.370
And in fact, a bunch of our
methods won't work on this.

00:46:33.370 --> 00:46:35.840
Now, sometimes, we actually
can solve problems like that.

00:46:35.840 --> 00:46:38.680
So, sorry, that was a
long and weird answer.

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:39.530
OK, let me just--

00:46:39.530 --> 00:46:40.780
I'm going to revise my answer.

00:46:40.780 --> 00:46:41.280
It's this.

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:43.150
It's because this
is the definition.

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:43.960
How about that?

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:46.120
But I won't say it
in the obnoxious way.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:53.150
And these equality
constraints, we

00:46:53.150 --> 00:46:55.010
will often collect
them and write them

00:46:55.010 --> 00:46:57.896
as just in a single
matrix form Ax equals b.

00:47:02.810 --> 00:47:05.000
Let's look at an example.

00:47:05.000 --> 00:47:06.060
It looks like this.

00:47:06.060 --> 00:47:07.970
Oh, and by the way,
let me point something

00:47:07.970 --> 00:47:11.360
out here very important.

00:47:11.360 --> 00:47:13.700
Here, a convex
optimization problem,

00:47:13.700 --> 00:47:18.770
we refer to its representation
through its objective function,

00:47:18.770 --> 00:47:22.340
its inequality functions,
and its equality constraint

00:47:22.340 --> 00:47:24.170
functions.

00:47:24.170 --> 00:47:30.290
We'll talk shortly about the
idea of an equivalent problem,

00:47:30.290 --> 00:47:33.740
but now it's actually,
literally, it is--

00:47:33.740 --> 00:47:36.110
when you have a problem,
it has attributes

00:47:36.110 --> 00:47:39.710
which are the objective
and things like that.

00:47:39.710 --> 00:47:42.620
To be the attribute
of being convex

00:47:42.620 --> 00:47:44.770
depends on how you
describe the problem.

00:47:44.770 --> 00:47:46.020
We'll get to that in a minute.

00:47:46.020 --> 00:47:47.520
In fact, this is
an example of that.

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:50.060
So here's a problem,
it's got two variables.

00:47:50.060 --> 00:47:52.490
I would like to minimize
x1 squared plus x2 squared.

00:47:52.490 --> 00:47:54.590
Well, that's just the
sum of the squares

00:47:54.590 --> 00:47:56.810
and that's like a nice
bowl that goes up.

00:47:56.810 --> 00:47:59.990
You what know what
it looks like.

00:47:59.990 --> 00:48:01.140
You have some constraints.

00:48:01.140 --> 00:48:04.950
You have f1 of x is x1 over 1
plus x2 squared is less than

00:48:04.950 --> 00:48:06.650
or equal to zero.

00:48:06.650 --> 00:48:09.980
Well, you can ask
yourself, is x1

00:48:09.980 --> 00:48:12.710
over 1 plus x2 squared convex?

00:48:12.710 --> 00:48:16.340
And the answer
would be it's not.

00:48:16.340 --> 00:48:19.670
This thing is not
a convex problem.

00:48:19.670 --> 00:48:21.682
And the last
constraint is that h--

00:48:21.682 --> 00:48:23.390
so I think you asked
a question about why

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:24.920
does it have to be
affine, and I said, well,

00:48:24.920 --> 00:48:25.970
it's the definition.

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:30.200
Here's an example where h
of x is for sure not affine.

00:48:30.200 --> 00:48:34.610
It says that x1 plus x2
squared has to be zero.

00:48:34.610 --> 00:48:36.180
So this problem fails.

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:41.700
And we can even walk
through the code.

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:44.990
If I took this problem
and I called it my prob,

00:48:44.990 --> 00:48:50.210
and I called the my prob
that is convex method on it,

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:53.930
then, here, we will walk
through what the code does.

00:48:53.930 --> 00:48:55.670
What it does like
this is it says,

00:48:55.670 --> 00:49:01.300
my problem dot objective,
that's going to be f zero,

00:49:01.300 --> 00:49:03.770
I call the dot is
convex method on it.

00:49:03.770 --> 00:49:07.820
And in this case, that
would evaluate to what?

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:08.570
True.

00:49:08.570 --> 00:49:10.160
It's true.

00:49:10.160 --> 00:49:11.270
No problem.

00:49:11.270 --> 00:49:13.460
Now, the second
part of implementing

00:49:13.460 --> 00:49:16.400
the is convex
method on a problem

00:49:16.400 --> 00:49:19.730
is to iterate over the
