00:54:03.920 --> 00:54:06.420
I mean, but it's probably
worthwhile for all of us

00:54:06.420 --> 00:54:10.560
to realize how absurd a
statement that sounds like.

00:54:10.560 --> 00:54:15.210
If you solve a problem with, I
don't know, 10,000 variables,

00:54:15.210 --> 00:54:19.530
and you'd say, I just worked
out the minimum fuel trajectory

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:21.360
to put your satellite
back on orbit

00:54:21.360 --> 00:54:22.890
where it's supposed to be.

00:54:22.890 --> 00:54:26.250
Perfectly reasonable person
would say, how could you

00:54:26.250 --> 00:54:27.390
say something like that?

00:54:27.390 --> 00:54:30.450
There are so many sequences
of thruster burns and things

00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:31.890
like that that
would get you back.

00:54:31.890 --> 00:54:33.138
How do you know?

00:54:33.138 --> 00:54:35.430
How could you possibly know
that this has minimum fuel?

00:54:35.430 --> 00:54:36.900
Do you see what I'm saying?

00:54:36.900 --> 00:54:40.140
By the way, we will--
after this little speech

00:54:40.140 --> 00:54:41.892
I quit, and we will
get you-- and that's

00:54:41.892 --> 00:54:43.350
all we're going to
say from now on.

00:54:43.350 --> 00:54:46.290
We'll just say, hey, this is
that, that's the optimal thing,

00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:47.410
and you get used to it.

00:54:47.410 --> 00:54:50.250
But it is probably worthwhile
to think about how strong

00:54:50.250 --> 00:54:54.450
a statement it is, and it
all comes back to this.

00:54:58.260 --> 00:54:59.205
And let's see why.

00:54:59.205 --> 00:55:00.580
I'm just going to
draw a picture.

00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:02.200
In fact, we're not
going to follow that.

00:55:02.200 --> 00:55:03.575
I'm just going to
draw a picture.

00:55:05.610 --> 00:55:09.300
Let's suppose that
you have a point here,

00:55:09.300 --> 00:55:10.860
it's locally optimal.

00:55:10.860 --> 00:55:13.980
So I'm going to
call that x local.

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:18.270
But let's suppose it's
not globally optimal.

00:55:18.270 --> 00:55:21.270
So that means, I'm going to
find another point that's

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:23.580
feasible and has a
lower objective value.

00:55:23.580 --> 00:55:27.540
And I'm just going to call
that over here x tilde.

00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:31.440
It could be x star,
an optimal point.

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:35.780
This is feasible,
locally optimal,

00:55:35.780 --> 00:55:38.290
and this has an objective
value less than that.

00:55:38.290 --> 00:55:39.730
Everybody got it?

00:55:39.730 --> 00:55:40.810
Now I do this.

00:55:40.810 --> 00:55:43.930
I draw the line
segment between them.

00:55:43.930 --> 00:55:48.950
Oh, on that line segment, are
all those points feasible?

00:55:48.950 --> 00:55:50.350
Why?

00:55:50.350 --> 00:55:52.760
It's a combination of the two.

00:55:52.760 --> 00:55:58.100
Because x local is
feasible, so is x tilde.

00:55:58.100 --> 00:55:59.990
The set of feasible
points is a convex set

00:55:59.990 --> 00:56:01.140
that's a line segment.

00:56:01.140 --> 00:56:04.760
So any point on
here is feasible.

00:56:04.760 --> 00:56:09.590
Now, I want to draw, as I move,
let me call my parameter t,

00:56:09.590 --> 00:56:12.410
so this is t equals
zero, t equals 1.

00:56:12.410 --> 00:56:15.140
And I want to draw
the objective value

00:56:15.140 --> 00:56:21.410
as I go from t equals
zero to t equals 1.

00:56:21.410 --> 00:56:23.240
And I know two points already.

00:56:23.240 --> 00:56:26.030
I'm going to draw f
zero of that point.

00:56:26.030 --> 00:56:31.040
This one, it starts at
f of x local here it

00:56:31.040 --> 00:56:34.130
ends at f zero of x
tilde by assumption

00:56:34.130 --> 00:56:36.260
that's better than f zero.

00:56:36.260 --> 00:56:39.800
So I don't know where it is,
but I'm going to draw it here.

00:56:39.800 --> 00:56:43.400
That's an f tilde of
x-- oh, what am I doing?

00:56:43.400 --> 00:56:44.750
F zero of x tilde.

00:56:44.750 --> 00:56:46.670
Everybody got this?

00:56:46.670 --> 00:56:50.220
Now, on this line
segment, I'm going

00:56:50.220 --> 00:56:52.860
to-- what I now have to do--
we know that when I draw that,

00:56:52.860 --> 00:56:56.780
it's a convex function, because
it's a convex function of t.

00:56:56.780 --> 00:57:00.710
This is restricting a convex
function to, in this case,

00:57:00.710 --> 00:57:03.740
an interval, but a
line and it's convex.

00:57:03.740 --> 00:57:08.490
I have to draw a convex function
that starts here and ends here.

00:57:08.490 --> 00:57:10.760
Everybody got that?

00:57:10.760 --> 00:57:13.680
Now when I do that, there's
lots of ways to do it.

00:57:13.680 --> 00:57:17.390
But no matter what
I do, it's going

00:57:17.390 --> 00:57:19.670
to go-- it has to go
down a little bit here.

00:57:22.750 --> 00:57:24.140
Now, we got a problem.

00:57:24.140 --> 00:57:26.440
What is the problem?

00:57:26.440 --> 00:57:31.010
Remember, somebody alleged
that x was locally optimal.

00:57:31.010 --> 00:57:33.290
This means whoever
said that was a liar.

00:57:33.290 --> 00:57:34.550
Let me tell you why.

00:57:34.550 --> 00:57:38.600
It says that there are points
on this line segment with really

00:57:38.600 --> 00:57:43.700
small t, which is it's
very close to x local,

00:57:43.700 --> 00:57:47.600
and the objective value
is less than x local.

00:57:47.600 --> 00:57:51.350
So x local was definitely
not a locally optimal point.

00:57:51.350 --> 00:57:53.330
Everybody got that?

00:57:53.330 --> 00:57:55.220
Honestly, I could write
it out as a proof.

00:57:55.220 --> 00:57:58.100
It's actually shorter
if I do, but the picture

00:57:58.100 --> 00:57:59.570
tells you everything.

00:57:59.570 --> 00:58:02.540
Does that make sense?

00:58:02.540 --> 00:58:04.280
So that's the idea there.

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:12.010
Now, here, we can give an
extremely simple optimality

00:58:12.010 --> 00:58:15.790
condition if f zero
is differentiable.

00:58:15.790 --> 00:58:16.820
It looks like this.

00:58:16.820 --> 00:58:23.140
It says that a point x is
optimal if it's feasible.

00:58:23.140 --> 00:58:27.010
And the following is
true, is that if x

00:58:27.010 --> 00:58:32.080
is the set of feasible points,
it says that the gradient of f

00:58:32.080 --> 00:58:33.740
transpose y minus
x, that's going

00:58:33.740 --> 00:58:35.380
to be bigger than or equal
to zero for all feasible y.

00:58:35.380 --> 00:58:36.580
And let's draw the picture.

00:58:36.580 --> 00:58:42.490
So these dashed curves are
the level curves of f zero.

00:58:42.490 --> 00:58:44.710
If you slide this way,
you're going downhill

00:58:44.710 --> 00:58:47.020
and you're getting
better values of f zero,

00:58:47.020 --> 00:58:50.342
which, by the semantics of
that being the objective,

00:58:50.342 --> 00:58:51.550
means you're getting happier.

00:58:51.550 --> 00:58:54.340
So you're getting happier
as you go down this way.

00:58:58.360 --> 00:59:01.000
But these are the
acceptable points.

00:59:01.000 --> 00:59:02.920
To solve the problem
visually, it just

00:59:02.920 --> 00:59:08.300
means you find the lowest level
curve that is on this thing,

00:59:08.300 --> 00:59:11.750
and that's going to
be this point here.

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:14.240
What happens there is
actually interesting.

00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:16.670
It just touches it.

00:59:16.670 --> 00:59:21.710
And the gradient, which would
be the outward normal, so

00:59:21.710 --> 00:59:25.490
the negative gradient like
this, points downhill.

00:59:25.490 --> 00:59:30.350
And that is actually orthogonal
to this tangent line here.

00:59:30.350 --> 00:59:32.990
So another way to say
it is that the gradient

00:59:32.990 --> 00:59:35.485
defines a supporting
hyperplane to the feasible set.

00:59:35.485 --> 00:59:37.235
That would use the
language we had before.

00:59:40.610 --> 00:59:43.070
I mean, I think
it's pretty basic.

00:59:43.070 --> 00:59:45.260
Weirdly, it looks complicated.

00:59:45.260 --> 00:59:48.410
Both proving both directions is
each like one line for each one

00:59:48.410 --> 00:59:50.600
because it's just silly.

00:59:50.600 --> 00:59:53.720
Some things look and sound
complicated and fancy,

00:59:53.720 --> 00:59:55.280
and they're not,
this is one of them.

00:59:58.070 --> 01:00:00.050
I think the picture
is pretty clear.

01:00:00.050 --> 01:00:01.430
So this is the picture.

01:00:07.620 --> 01:00:12.840
Now, we can instantiate that
in various versions of it.

01:00:12.840 --> 01:00:14.460
Here's one version.

01:00:14.460 --> 01:00:16.140
Suppose there's
no constraint set.

01:00:16.140 --> 01:00:18.600
If there's no constraint
set, then what we're saying

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:20.490
is x is all of our n.

01:00:20.490 --> 01:00:24.060
And this inequality has
to hold for all our n.

01:00:24.060 --> 01:00:27.540
Any comment about that?

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:31.770
If I tell you this inequality
holds for any vector y,

01:00:31.770 --> 01:00:34.110
the only possible
way that can happen

01:00:34.110 --> 01:00:36.700
is this gradient has to vanish.

01:00:36.700 --> 01:00:40.050
Because if this
gradient didn't vanish,

01:00:40.050 --> 01:00:43.980
then I would pick y to be
minus that gradient multiplied

01:00:43.980 --> 01:00:47.190
by your favorite
extremely large number.

01:00:47.190 --> 01:00:49.800
And then this thing here
would definitely not

01:00:49.800 --> 01:00:51.450
be bigger than or equal to zero.

01:00:51.450 --> 01:00:53.220
Everybody following that?

01:00:53.220 --> 01:00:56.610
So that would be the idea there.

01:00:56.610 --> 01:00:59.280
Basically, this
recovers, I guess,

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:04.620
what you know from your
Calculus 2 or whatever course.

01:01:04.620 --> 01:01:08.130
It says that in an
unconstrained problem,

01:01:08.130 --> 01:01:11.850
if f zero is differentiable,
the gradient vanishing is what

01:01:11.850 --> 01:01:13.777
it means to solve the problem.

01:01:13.777 --> 01:01:16.110
By the way, this is a bit
different from your Calculus 2

01:01:16.110 --> 01:01:16.720
course.

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:18.045
Let me tell you why.

01:01:18.045 --> 01:01:20.670
In your Calculus 2 course, when
you said the gradient vanished,

01:01:20.670 --> 01:01:23.100
all it meant was that
it's at a stationary point

01:01:23.100 --> 01:01:23.785
of the function.

01:01:23.785 --> 01:01:26.160
It could have been a saddle
point, it could be a maximum,

01:01:26.160 --> 01:01:28.480
it could be a local
minimum, local maximum,

01:01:28.480 --> 01:01:29.590
all this kind of stuff.

01:01:29.590 --> 01:01:33.580
Here, because we also
know f zero is convex,

01:01:33.580 --> 01:01:37.208
it's actually globally optimal.

01:01:37.208 --> 01:01:39.250
And we don't even have to
say that globally, it's

01:01:39.250 --> 01:01:41.230
just optimal.

01:01:41.230 --> 01:01:44.120
So that's it.

01:01:44.120 --> 01:01:48.440
So what that says is
when in Calculus 2

01:01:48.440 --> 01:01:52.010
you were taught how to do
baby optimization stuff which

01:01:52.010 --> 01:01:53.180
was a behavioral thing.

01:01:53.180 --> 01:01:55.550
And it basically said you
take the function, set

01:01:55.550 --> 01:01:58.040
the gradient equal to
zero, and then something

01:01:58.040 --> 01:01:59.870
happens after that.

01:01:59.870 --> 01:02:03.910
That's my general
memory of this.

01:02:03.910 --> 01:02:05.700
It turns out for
convex functions,

01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:08.280
that's exactly correct.

01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:10.200
Oh, and not only that,
you're going to get--

01:02:10.200 --> 01:02:15.300
you will get a minimizer--
an optimal point period.

01:02:15.300 --> 01:02:19.350
None of this weird legal
boilerplate or footnotes

01:02:19.350 --> 01:02:23.342
that says, oh, it might be a
maximum, it might be a minimum,

01:02:23.342 --> 01:02:25.800
oh it might be a saddle point,
it might be a local minimum,

01:02:25.800 --> 01:02:26.640
it might be a local.

01:02:26.640 --> 01:02:28.082
None of that boilerplate.

01:02:28.082 --> 01:02:29.165
It's just optimal, period.

01:02:33.948 --> 01:02:36.240
We'll look at optimality
conditions later in the class,

01:02:36.240 --> 01:02:38.573
but some of them are just
very easy to work out directly

01:02:38.573 --> 01:02:40.860
from this general principle.

01:02:40.860 --> 01:02:43.280
So if you have an equality
constraint problem,

01:02:43.280 --> 01:02:44.780
what happens is you
want to minimize

01:02:44.780 --> 01:02:47.960
f zero of x subject
to Ax equals b.

01:02:47.960 --> 01:02:49.933
You're optimal, you
have to be feasible,

01:02:49.933 --> 01:02:51.600
you have to be in the
domain, of course.

01:02:51.600 --> 01:02:53.540
And then you can
work out what it

01:02:53.540 --> 01:02:55.100
means for that
gradient inequality

01:02:55.100 --> 01:02:56.997
to hold for an affine set.

01:02:56.997 --> 01:02:59.330
And when you do, it's going
to end up looking like this.

01:02:59.330 --> 01:03:01.880
That's also from your
Calculus 2 class because these

01:03:01.880 --> 01:03:04.070
are Lagrange multipliers.

01:03:04.070 --> 01:03:07.580
We are going to cover that in
about a week and a half, two

01:03:07.580 --> 01:03:11.540
weeks, in a theory
called duality.

01:03:11.540 --> 01:03:16.280
After which, I believe you
will actually understand it.

01:03:16.280 --> 01:03:19.520
Because I don't mind admitting
I saw things about Lagrange

01:03:19.520 --> 01:03:24.560
multipliers two or three times
and had no idea what they meant

01:03:24.560 --> 01:03:25.310
did.

01:03:25.310 --> 01:03:26.700
Why would you do this?

01:03:26.700 --> 01:03:28.910
Why would you do that?

01:03:28.910 --> 01:03:32.210
Anyway, you don't know what
I'm talking about probably.

01:03:32.210 --> 01:03:33.780
I mean, unless
you're in-- there are

01:03:33.780 --> 01:03:35.790
fields where this does come up.

01:03:35.790 --> 01:03:37.320
Probably, if you're
in economics,

01:03:37.320 --> 01:03:39.240
maybe if you're in
mechanical engineering,

01:03:39.240 --> 01:03:42.360
you actually do have a feel for
it because it comes up there.

01:03:42.360 --> 01:03:44.230
Maybe in statistics,
I don't know.

01:03:44.230 --> 01:03:47.255
But the point is we'll
cover this in detail later.

01:03:49.800 --> 01:03:54.697
You can work out other cases,
like minimize a convex function

01:03:54.697 --> 01:03:56.280
over the non-negative
orthant, and you

01:03:56.280 --> 01:03:57.405
can work out what it means.

01:03:57.405 --> 01:04:02.430
And actually, it's also
makes perfect sense.

01:04:02.430 --> 01:04:05.310
It would mean that if
I minimize a convex

01:04:05.310 --> 01:04:10.800
function over non-negative
orthant, let me just draw this.

01:04:10.800 --> 01:04:13.260
I want to minimize the
convex function over here,

01:04:13.260 --> 01:04:15.550
and here's an example here.

01:04:15.550 --> 01:04:17.880
Here are some sublevel sets.

01:04:17.880 --> 01:04:19.920
Like this.

01:04:19.920 --> 01:04:21.540
Like that, here's some--

01:04:21.540 --> 01:04:26.550
whoops, these are
sublevel sets, like that.

01:04:26.550 --> 01:04:28.560
That's the optimal point.

01:04:28.560 --> 01:04:32.250
And we can check this
because what happens here--

01:04:32.250 --> 01:04:35.460
and this is just very intuitive.

01:04:35.460 --> 01:04:41.260
What it says is that
this partial f zero

01:04:41.260 --> 01:04:45.130
partial x2, that has to vanish.

01:04:45.130 --> 01:04:47.020
If it didn't vanish,
we'd be in trouble.

01:04:47.020 --> 01:04:49.570
Because I could slide
up and down here

01:04:49.570 --> 01:04:51.380
in the direction of
the negative gradient

01:04:51.380 --> 01:04:53.463
and actually get a slightly
better objective value

01:04:53.463 --> 01:04:57.640
while staying feasible, in
which case, it was not optimal.

01:04:57.640 --> 01:05:00.130
But I'm slammed up against
the boundary of the constraint

01:05:00.130 --> 01:05:01.960
here.

01:05:01.960 --> 01:05:04.210
I would love to go
in that direction.

01:05:04.210 --> 01:05:06.430
I'd love to because my
objective would go down.

01:05:06.430 --> 01:05:08.722
The problem is I'm at the
boundary of the feasible set,

01:05:08.722 --> 01:05:10.780
and I can't.

01:05:10.780 --> 01:05:12.385
Anyway, when you work this out--

01:05:12.385 --> 01:05:15.370
you just can just work
this out algebraically

01:05:15.370 --> 01:05:18.890
and you get something
like that condition there.

01:05:18.890 --> 01:05:21.253
So anyway, we'll come back
to optimality conditions

01:05:21.253 --> 01:05:22.045
in about two weeks.

01:05:24.405 --> 01:05:26.530
Now, we're going to talk
about equivalent problems.

01:05:26.530 --> 01:05:28.030
I already said a
little bit about it

01:05:28.030 --> 01:05:30.850
earlier, we actually saw two
equivalent problems, in fact,

01:05:30.850 --> 01:05:31.540
earlier.

01:05:31.540 --> 01:05:33.457
Now, we're going to talk
about it because it's

01:05:33.457 --> 01:05:36.797
an official idea we have.

01:05:36.797 --> 01:05:38.380
Two problems-- and
we're going to make

01:05:38.380 --> 01:05:39.670
it informal in this class.

01:05:39.670 --> 01:05:42.730
In CS theory, it's not informal.

01:05:42.730 --> 01:05:46.060
Here it is, there it's called
a reduction by the way.

01:05:46.060 --> 01:05:49.510
So here, it's going
to be informal.

01:05:49.510 --> 01:05:52.840
And we say, basically,
if one problem

01:05:52.840 --> 01:05:55.930
is equivalent to another,
if from a solution of one

01:05:55.930 --> 01:06:00.820
I can create a solution of
the other and vice versa.

01:06:00.820 --> 01:06:02.470
So that's what that means.

01:06:02.470 --> 01:06:05.845
That's exactly the idea of a
reduction in computer science.

01:06:09.665 --> 01:06:12.040
So there's a whole bunch of
them that you should probably

01:06:12.040 --> 01:06:13.450
know about.

01:06:13.450 --> 01:06:14.950
In fact, a lot of
the software tools

01:06:14.950 --> 01:06:16.533
you're going to use
actually are going

01:06:16.533 --> 01:06:19.750
to use on your behalf
many reductions.

01:06:19.750 --> 01:06:21.110
Sorry, OK, whatever.

01:06:21.110 --> 01:06:21.610
Sorry.

01:06:21.610 --> 01:06:23.890
They'll use many
equivalences, which

01:06:23.890 --> 01:06:25.810
are reductions in
computer science

01:06:25.810 --> 01:06:28.240
that you don't
need to know about.

01:06:28.240 --> 01:06:30.340
But you need to know
about some of them

01:06:30.340 --> 01:06:32.923
because they're actually-- and
I'll just mention some of them,

01:06:32.923 --> 01:06:35.410
a lot of them are generic,
but let's look at some.

01:06:35.410 --> 01:06:36.790
Here's one.

01:06:36.790 --> 01:06:39.250
Here's a problem with
equality constraints.

01:06:41.890 --> 01:06:45.310
We can just basically eliminate
the equality constraints,

01:06:45.310 --> 01:06:47.620
I can take a new variable.

01:06:47.620 --> 01:06:50.180
And the first thing
I do is I find

01:06:50.180 --> 01:06:54.040
a vector x zero that
satisfies Ax equals b,

01:06:54.040 --> 01:07:00.700
and I find a matrix whose range
is exactly the null space of A.

01:07:00.700 --> 01:07:03.940
What that tells you is
that Ax is equal to b, if,

01:07:03.940 --> 01:07:08.390
and only, if you have the
form x equals fz plus x zero.

01:07:08.390 --> 01:07:14.310
X zero and f, we can
compute from a and b.

01:07:14.310 --> 01:07:15.420
We compute them.

01:07:15.420 --> 01:07:18.510
I won't go into how we do
that, lots of ways to do that.

01:07:18.510 --> 01:07:19.430
So you do that.

01:07:19.430 --> 01:07:22.670
If you do this,
then this problem

01:07:22.670 --> 01:07:25.880
is equivalent to that problem.

01:07:25.880 --> 01:07:28.880
By the way, they're
not equal at all.

01:07:28.880 --> 01:07:31.250
I mean, for one thing at a
very gross level, it's not.

01:07:31.250 --> 01:07:34.760
If you say this
problem, that equality--

01:07:34.760 --> 01:07:39.230
if you say what is the
length of this problem,

01:07:39.230 --> 01:07:41.960
that equality constraints,
it's going to be however many,

01:07:41.960 --> 01:07:45.500
let's say this p in p equality
constraints, it'll be p.

01:07:45.500 --> 01:07:48.740
Here, when I say
this second problem,

01:07:48.740 --> 01:07:51.830
that equality constraints,
if I say length of that,

01:07:51.830 --> 01:07:55.610
it's a list, it's an empty
list, and so it's zero.

01:07:55.610 --> 01:07:58.370
End of story, these are not
equal, but they are equivalent.

01:08:02.210 --> 01:08:04.760
In this case, the p star
of the two problems,

01:08:04.760 --> 01:08:08.050
the optimal value is the same.

01:08:08.050 --> 01:08:10.550
To explain what you do,
you say like, well, you

01:08:10.550 --> 01:08:11.580
want to solve this.

01:08:11.580 --> 01:08:15.410
So what we do is first we
compute this f and the x zero,

01:08:15.410 --> 01:08:16.729
then we solve this problem.

01:08:16.729 --> 01:08:21.380
Now, z has nothing to do
with this problem at all.

01:08:21.380 --> 01:08:24.319
But then what I simply do
is I return in this problem,

01:08:24.319 --> 01:08:27.439
I return fz star plus x zero.

01:08:27.439 --> 01:08:29.510
And that's going to
be the solution here.

01:08:29.510 --> 01:08:32.450
So this actually also has
a very important like,

01:08:32.450 --> 01:08:36.380
if someone comes up with a super
fancy solver for a problem,

01:08:36.380 --> 01:08:38.450
they go, it's awesome.

01:08:38.450 --> 01:08:39.950
It's super fast.

01:08:39.950 --> 01:08:41.720
Whatever it is,
it doesn't matter.

01:08:41.720 --> 01:08:44.420
Then you say only
minor problem, it

01:08:44.420 --> 01:08:46.550
doesn't support
equality constraints.

01:08:46.550 --> 01:08:48.229
Everybody got that?

01:08:48.229 --> 01:08:50.390
This actually, now,
it's like no problem,

01:08:50.390 --> 01:08:52.250
I just write a
wrapper around it.

01:08:52.250 --> 01:08:54.470
And my wrapper takes
problems like this,

01:08:54.470 --> 01:08:56.180
calculates f and x zero.

01:08:56.180 --> 01:08:58.370
We could write the whole
wrapper beautifully.

01:08:58.370 --> 01:09:01.385
The first thing you do is you
attempt to solve Ax equals b.

01:09:01.385 --> 01:09:03.859
What if b is not
in the range of a?

01:09:03.859 --> 01:09:06.729
What do I do?

01:09:06.729 --> 01:09:10.000
I returned that your
problem is infeasible.

01:09:10.000 --> 01:09:10.630
Period.

01:09:10.630 --> 01:09:12.255
And then I'll have
to do anything else.

01:09:12.255 --> 01:09:14.800
If it's feasible, I'm
going to find an x zero

01:09:14.800 --> 01:09:16.078
and I'm going to find this f.

01:09:16.078 --> 01:09:17.620
I'm going to solve
this problem using

01:09:17.620 --> 01:09:19.390
this person's fancy solver.

01:09:19.390 --> 01:09:22.899
I will then quietly
return fz plus x zero

01:09:22.899 --> 01:09:25.569
and that will, in fact, be
a solution of this problem.

01:09:25.569 --> 01:09:27.700
Everybody got it?

01:09:27.700 --> 01:09:30.370
That's called eliminating
equality constraints.

01:09:30.370 --> 01:09:35.439
And actually, there's a whole
lot of confusion about that.

01:09:35.439 --> 01:09:39.819
I think some very naive
people think this is great.

01:09:39.819 --> 01:09:42.220
And you say, why?

01:09:42.220 --> 01:09:43.510
And here would be the story.

01:09:43.510 --> 01:09:45.821
It's naive, it's totally
wrong, but here's the story.

01:09:45.821 --> 01:09:48.279
I mean, it's plausible if you
don't know what you're doing.

01:09:48.279 --> 01:09:49.300
Here it is.

01:09:49.300 --> 01:09:51.279
They say, well, I have
this problem here,

01:09:51.279 --> 01:09:53.630
x has dimension 100.

01:09:53.630 --> 01:09:55.520
And I have 50
equality constraints.

01:09:55.520 --> 01:09:56.750
Everybody got that?

01:09:56.750 --> 01:09:59.780
Now, down here, that's
assuming that's like that's got

01:09:59.780 --> 01:10:00.780
full rank and all that.

01:10:00.780 --> 01:10:03.440
So down here, z has ranked 50.

01:10:03.440 --> 01:10:09.200
Sorry, dimension 50, because if
I have 100 dimensional space,

01:10:09.200 --> 01:10:11.810
I have 50 equality constraints,
everything's full rank, z

01:10:11.810 --> 01:10:13.460
is going to have dimension 50.

01:10:13.460 --> 01:10:14.960
And then someone
says, oh, that's

01:10:14.960 --> 01:10:16.940
a better problem than
that one to solve.

01:10:16.940 --> 01:10:19.100
And you go, why?

01:10:19.100 --> 01:10:20.372
And they go, Hello?

01:10:20.372 --> 01:10:21.830
Wouldn't you rather
solve a problem

01:10:21.830 --> 01:10:23.880
with 50 variables than 100?

01:10:23.880 --> 01:10:25.130
Everybody see what I'm saying?

01:10:25.130 --> 01:10:28.670
It sounds plausible, but it's
completely and totally wrong,

01:10:28.670 --> 01:10:31.520
which you will know later.

01:10:31.520 --> 01:10:34.730
In fact, it could be
the exact opposite

01:10:34.730 --> 01:10:36.530
that you would much
rather solve a problem

01:10:36.530 --> 01:10:38.160
with 100 variables than 50.

01:10:38.160 --> 01:10:40.160
I'm getting ahead of
myself, but we'll come back

01:10:40.160 --> 01:10:41.285
to that later in the class.

01:10:44.480 --> 01:10:47.770
You can do the opposite, yes.

01:10:47.770 --> 01:10:50.300
How do you actually
compute x minus f zero?

01:10:50.300 --> 01:10:51.890
Oh, that's linear algebra.

01:10:51.890 --> 01:10:54.480
Yeah, that's a standard
linear algebra.

01:10:54.480 --> 01:10:55.350
I don't know.

01:10:55.350 --> 01:10:57.788
I do a QR factorization
of like f or--

01:10:57.788 --> 01:10:59.205
I don't know,
something like that.

01:11:03.820 --> 01:11:06.280
I mean, I'd be happy to
tell you later or something,

01:11:06.280 --> 01:11:08.155
but that's just linear algebra.

01:11:13.260 --> 01:11:16.920
Next up is the opposite of
eliminating is introducing

01:11:16.920 --> 01:11:18.930
equality constraints.

01:11:18.930 --> 01:11:23.870
Weirdly, this
sounds really dumb.

01:11:23.870 --> 01:11:28.020
It's unbelievably useful,
and we will see that later.

01:11:28.020 --> 01:11:29.840
So how does that work?

01:11:29.840 --> 01:11:31.460
You start with a
problem like that

01:11:31.460 --> 01:11:33.680
and you introduce a new
variable and you and you

01:11:33.680 --> 01:11:37.510
introduce equality constraints.

01:11:37.510 --> 01:11:40.520
What's hilarious about
this is if someone says,

01:11:40.520 --> 01:11:43.780
oh, thanks for your
problem, I just made you

01:11:43.780 --> 01:11:45.770
an equivalent problem.

01:11:45.770 --> 01:11:48.950
Oh, and by the way, you have to
check that these equivalences

01:11:48.950 --> 01:11:51.620
play nice with convexity.

01:11:51.620 --> 01:11:55.520
I didn't do that for the other
one, so let's go back and look.

01:11:55.520 --> 01:11:58.280
Here, if that's
a convex problem,

01:11:58.280 --> 01:11:59.540
we have to check this is.

01:11:59.540 --> 01:12:00.515
How come this is?

01:12:03.540 --> 01:12:06.220
How come?

01:12:06.220 --> 01:12:09.760
Because this function is convex,
because it's a convex function

01:12:09.760 --> 01:12:12.040
precomposed with
an affine function.

01:12:12.040 --> 01:12:14.270
These are convex
for the same reason.

01:12:14.270 --> 01:12:16.218
So we do have to say
that-- at this point,

01:12:16.218 --> 01:12:17.260
we're not even saying it.

01:12:20.000 --> 01:12:21.430
So you un-eliminate here.

01:12:21.430 --> 01:12:23.847
Now, what's hilarious about
this is someone says, oh yeah,

01:12:23.847 --> 01:12:25.805
I just converted your
problem to a new problem.

01:12:25.805 --> 01:12:26.980
And you go, oh, thank you.

01:12:26.980 --> 01:12:27.670
That's great.

01:12:27.670 --> 01:12:29.720
Tell me about the new
problem and you go well,

01:12:29.720 --> 01:12:31.870
it's got more variables.

01:12:31.870 --> 01:12:33.600
You're like, OK.

01:12:33.600 --> 01:12:35.460
And you go, oh, by
the way, your problem

01:12:35.460 --> 01:12:37.002
didn't have any
equality constraints,

01:12:37.002 --> 01:12:38.570
now you have a bunch of them.

01:12:38.570 --> 01:12:40.568
This doesn't sound
good, this story

01:12:40.568 --> 01:12:42.110
doesn't sound like
it's going anyway.

01:12:42.110 --> 01:12:46.220
Believe it or not, it's going
to be super useful this idea.

01:12:48.770 --> 01:12:51.290
Here's one you can--
this is a famous one.

01:12:51.290 --> 01:12:53.720
These are all methods
that a lot of people

01:12:53.720 --> 01:12:58.490
know and would learn about
by hand, actually literally,

01:12:58.490 --> 01:13:00.320
since the 1940s.

01:13:00.320 --> 01:13:01.475
So here's one.

01:13:05.420 --> 01:13:07.760
You can take a linear
inequality and introduce

01:13:07.760 --> 01:13:10.130
what's called a slack
variable, that's

01:13:10.130 --> 01:13:15.260
a standard term, not in
math, but, for example,

01:13:15.260 --> 01:13:18.080
in optimization.

01:13:18.080 --> 01:13:20.683
These are just very simple
and dumb these little

01:13:20.683 --> 01:13:21.350
transformations.

01:13:21.350 --> 01:13:23.960
But this one says,
we rewrite this

01:13:23.960 --> 01:13:26.517
as AI transpose x
plus Si equals bi,

01:13:26.517 --> 01:13:28.100
where Si is a number
bigger than zero,

01:13:28.100 --> 01:13:30.260
and that's called the slack.

01:13:30.260 --> 01:13:32.630
Slack makes perfect sense.

01:13:32.630 --> 01:13:35.450
It means if you're right
up against is, S is zero.

01:13:35.450 --> 01:13:39.270
If S is positive, it means
this constraint is satisfied

01:13:39.270 --> 01:13:41.430
with strict inequality.

01:13:41.430 --> 01:13:44.580
And slack comes from
the idea of a chain

01:13:44.580 --> 01:13:46.560
or a rope between two things.

01:13:46.560 --> 01:13:49.560
The constraint is if I put a
steel cable between two things

01:13:49.560 --> 01:13:53.100
and I make it a meter long, then
those two things cannot be more

01:13:53.100 --> 01:13:55.380
than a meter apart.

01:13:55.380 --> 01:14:00.360
If they are exactly a meter
apart, then that chain is taut,

01:14:00.360 --> 01:14:02.280
that's not a word
in optimization.

01:14:02.280 --> 01:14:06.780
But if they're less than a
meter apart, then the chain,

01:14:06.780 --> 01:14:10.710
there's zero tension in the
chain, and then it's slack.

01:14:10.710 --> 01:14:14.100
And that word did make
it into optimization.

01:14:14.100 --> 01:14:16.980
So if you're curious,
that's what it means.

01:14:16.980 --> 01:14:21.510
Slack rope or slack
cable or something.

01:14:21.510 --> 01:14:25.350
So this is called
introducing slack variables.

01:14:25.350 --> 01:14:26.280
We'll show a couple.

01:14:26.280 --> 01:14:30.270
This one is also interesting.

01:14:30.270 --> 01:14:32.452
I have my standard form
problem and I can rewrite it

01:14:32.452 --> 01:14:33.660
this way, which is hilarious.

01:14:33.660 --> 01:14:36.090
I introduce a new variable.

01:14:36.090 --> 01:14:37.410
Now my variables are xt.

01:14:37.410 --> 01:14:41.730
Now when you hear xt, you should
be thinking graph, epigraph,

01:14:41.730 --> 01:14:43.320
hypergraph, these
kinds of things,

01:14:43.320 --> 01:14:45.030
because that's what
it generally means

01:14:45.030 --> 01:14:48.630
when you have a variable
and a comma and scalar.

01:14:48.630 --> 01:14:53.520
Here, this constraint
here simply says that xt,

01:14:53.520 --> 01:14:56.070
the pair xt is in the epigraph.

01:14:56.070 --> 01:14:57.150
I mean, it's silly.

01:14:57.150 --> 01:14:59.700
This just says t is
bigger than the objective.

01:14:59.700 --> 01:15:03.480
And then it says, you
minimize t subject to this.

01:15:03.480 --> 01:15:05.730
Now, here's what's
cool about this.

01:15:05.730 --> 01:15:07.620
Some people say this
in a very fancy way.

01:15:07.620 --> 01:15:10.470
They say things like,
for convex optimization,

01:15:10.470 --> 01:15:13.570
a linear objective is universal.

01:15:13.570 --> 01:15:16.050
I don't even know
what that means.

01:15:16.050 --> 01:15:19.260
So it says, without
loss of generality--

01:15:19.260 --> 01:15:23.610
well, you have to-- if you
can handle this constraint

01:15:23.610 --> 01:15:27.000
and then you have a linear
objective, you're good to go.

01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:30.060
You just solve the
problem in epigraph form.

01:15:30.060 --> 01:15:32.640
Make sense?

01:15:32.640 --> 01:15:35.030
That's why a lot of
times if you'd say--

01:15:35.030 --> 01:15:38.580
you'd look at you'd say,
oh, I went to Google.

01:15:38.580 --> 01:15:41.380
I looked for code
to solve this thing.

01:15:41.380 --> 01:15:43.602
And it's not there
because it's what

01:15:43.602 --> 01:15:45.060
you see-- the only
thing I can find

01:15:45.060 --> 01:15:46.852
has a linear-- can
solve a linear objective

01:15:46.852 --> 01:15:49.590
and my problem has like
a nonlinear objective.

01:15:49.590 --> 01:15:51.090
And the reason is
people are just

01:15:51.090 --> 01:15:53.175
expecting you to put
something into epigraph form.

01:15:57.173 --> 01:15:58.590
Other things you
can do are things

01:15:58.590 --> 01:16:00.870
like selective minimization.

01:16:00.870 --> 01:16:02.280
You have two variables here.

01:16:02.280 --> 01:16:05.310
I could actually eliminate x2.

01:16:05.310 --> 01:16:11.167
Suppose I can analytically
minimize over f zero over x2,

01:16:11.167 --> 01:16:13.500
then I can do that and I have
a problem that looks like.

01:16:13.500 --> 01:16:15.070
This would be the
case, for example,

01:16:15.070 --> 01:16:17.910
if this function
were quadratic in x2,

01:16:17.910 --> 01:16:21.120
I can minimize a
quadratic analytically.

01:16:21.120 --> 01:16:23.310
I could do that, and
then I eliminate x2.

01:16:23.310 --> 01:16:25.740
And this is basically
like-- this is basically

01:16:25.740 --> 01:16:27.540
part of dynamic programming.

01:16:27.540 --> 01:16:29.580
That's what would happen
in dynamic programming.

01:16:29.580 --> 01:16:32.760
If you want to
find a good choice

01:16:32.760 --> 01:16:35.910
of an input over some
sequence, you start at the end,

01:16:35.910 --> 01:16:40.020
you minimize over the last one,
and then there this function

01:16:40.020 --> 01:16:41.580
is called like
the value function

01:16:41.580 --> 01:16:43.620
or something like that.

01:16:43.620 --> 01:16:46.260
And you just keep
going and it all works.

01:16:46.260 --> 01:16:50.110
This one also, all of these
play nice with convexity.

01:16:50.110 --> 01:16:54.390
So here, the reason f
zero tilde is convex

01:16:54.390 --> 01:16:57.240
is because it is partial
minimization of f

01:16:57.240 --> 01:17:01.380
zero with respect
to the variable x2.

01:17:01.380 --> 01:17:02.790
Everybody got that?

01:17:02.790 --> 01:17:07.920
So all the rules you saw before
were now using for problems.

01:17:11.550 --> 01:17:15.000
I'll say a little bit about
quasi convex optimization.

01:17:15.000 --> 01:17:19.380
If I have convex inequality
constraints, linear equality

01:17:19.380 --> 01:17:22.350
constraints, and I minimize
a quasi convex function,

01:17:22.350 --> 01:17:25.050
that's called quasi
convex optimization.

01:17:25.050 --> 01:17:28.320
You have to be very careful
here because, for example, this

01:17:28.320 --> 01:17:30.790
is for sure a quasi
convex function.

01:17:30.790 --> 01:17:33.420
And by the way, it's
actually really flat here.

01:17:33.420 --> 01:17:36.180
It's not that it's
visually flat, it's flat.

01:17:36.180 --> 01:17:37.920
In this one and then
it keeps going up.

01:17:37.920 --> 01:17:40.130
This quasi convex.

01:17:40.130 --> 01:17:44.210
That point is for
sure locally optimal

01:17:44.210 --> 01:17:46.160
because you wiggle a
little bit left and right

01:17:46.160 --> 01:17:49.610
and the objective
does not go down.

01:17:49.610 --> 01:17:53.010
But it is clearly not the
optimum, which is here.

01:17:53.010 --> 01:17:56.900
So some things don't
hold in this case.

01:17:56.900 --> 01:17:59.900
But it turns out we can solve
these using convex optimization

01:17:59.900 --> 01:18:00.890
pretty easily.

01:18:00.890 --> 01:18:08.270
And I'll show this trick and
then we'll quit for today.

01:18:08.270 --> 01:18:11.180
If f zero is quasi
convex, then what you do

01:18:11.180 --> 01:18:14.360
is you find a parametrized
set of convex functions

01:18:14.360 --> 01:18:16.110
which have the following form.

01:18:16.110 --> 01:18:20.780
This defines a convex
set in x for each t.

01:18:20.780 --> 01:18:23.540
That's actually what it
means to be quasi convex.

01:18:23.540 --> 01:18:26.870
So what I do is I express
that as a convex function

01:18:26.870 --> 01:18:28.610
by t being less than zero.

01:18:28.610 --> 01:18:32.090
And here's an example,
the ratio of a convex

01:18:32.090 --> 01:18:35.750
to a concave positive--

01:18:35.750 --> 01:18:41.730
non-negative convex divided by
a concave positive function.

01:18:41.730 --> 01:18:43.245
This is actually quasi convex.

01:18:45.930 --> 01:18:48.790
Well, how do I check that?

01:18:48.790 --> 01:18:51.450
Well, I want to know
what happens if p of x--

01:18:51.450 --> 01:18:53.370
I want to look at the
sublevel set, which

01:18:53.370 --> 01:18:55.545
is p of x over q of x is
less than or equal to t.

01:18:55.545 --> 01:18:58.860
Q is positive, I multiply
it on the other side,

01:18:58.860 --> 01:19:01.770
and I got p of x is
less than t q of x.

01:19:01.770 --> 01:19:03.840
Everybody following this?

01:19:03.840 --> 01:19:07.680
I pull it over and I
get p minus t q of x.

01:19:07.680 --> 01:19:09.570
If this is less than
or equal to zero,

01:19:09.570 --> 01:19:11.295
then this thing is less than t.

01:19:14.200 --> 01:19:15.710
That's a convex function.

01:19:15.710 --> 01:19:20.380
So it's a convex in x not
jointly in x and t, But in x.

01:19:20.380 --> 01:19:23.437
For each t positive,
non-negative,

01:19:23.437 --> 01:19:24.520
this is a convex function.

01:19:28.270 --> 01:19:29.920
Actually, 'what
this says is I have

01:19:29.920 --> 01:19:34.780
a specific way of
seeing if I can find

01:19:34.780 --> 01:19:37.160
a point that has that value.

01:19:37.160 --> 01:19:40.270
And then what we do
is just bisection.

01:19:40.270 --> 01:19:44.830
I'll quit very briefly,
but this is the idea.

01:19:44.830 --> 01:19:49.300
What you do is you simply
solve a feasibility problem.

01:19:49.300 --> 01:19:53.620
This one basically says, can you
find a point where f zero of x

01:19:53.620 --> 01:19:54.490
is less than t?

01:19:54.490 --> 01:19:56.260
That's this.

01:19:56.260 --> 01:19:57.730
These are the constraints.

01:19:57.730 --> 01:20:00.040
If you can, then
you found a point

01:20:00.040 --> 01:20:02.980
and then you know for
sure the optimal p star

01:20:02.980 --> 01:20:04.430
